  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "inter-graph-edge-semantics-declaration",
      "type": "declaration",
      "label": "Inter-Graph Edge Semantics",
      "position": {
        "x": -520,
        "y": -440
      },
      "width": 400,
      "height": 250,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "inter-graph-edge-semantics",
          "nodeId": "inter-graph-edge-semantics",
          "name": "Inter-Graph Edge Semantics",
          "version": "0.3.0",
          "description": "A recursive Q&A contract for graph-local edges, Bridge-projected handles, and ordinary workspace-owned edges between rendered graph-nodes."
        },
        "intent": {
          "kind": "investigation",
          "scope": "local"
        },
        "document": {
          "url": "github://twilite-zone/public/tasks/inter-graph-edge-semantics/root.node"
        },
        "declaration": {
          "kind": "investigation",
          "targetMode": "artifact",
          "defaultSurfaceId": "",
          "surfaces": []
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "problem",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "What feels off?",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": -440
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 330,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#241d32",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# What feels off?\n\nWe are treating several workspace lines as if they were interchangeable:\n\n- a Portal following a Port\n- an export/import Bridge pair\n- an authored edge crossing a graph-document seam\n- an edge between two rendered semantic nodes\n- a derived workspace route\n\nThey may look similar, but they do not necessarily have the same identity or authority."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-visible-line",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q1 · Is every workspace line an edge?",
      "position": {
        "x": -900,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nIs every line drawn between node regions a graph edge?\n\n## Answer\n\nNo. A Portal binding visualizes traversal or shared presentation. A relationship drawn between projected Bridge handles is a regular edge owned by the containing workspace graph. Geometry alone does not decide which one it is."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-bridge",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q2 · Is a Bridge an edge?",
      "position": {
        "x": -440,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhere do Bridges come from?\n\n## Answer\n\nThe parent edge is primary. When an endpoint graph-node unfolds, Twilite extrapolates an export Bridge at the source and an import Bridge at the target. Each is a deterministic node projection carrying the parent edge ID."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-owner",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q3 · Who owns edge type?",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhere does semantic edge-class identity belong?\n\n## Answer\n\nOn the edge at the graph level that owns it. An inner graph owns its local edge to a Bridge. The workspace graph owns its edge between rendered graph-nodes. A Bridge constrains the classes accepted by its projected handle."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-one-or-two",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q4 · One edge or two?",
      "position": {
        "x": 480,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhen a relationship appears continuous across graph levels, is it one edge or several?\n\n## Answer\n\nIt is a recursive composition of ordinary edges. Each edge has one owning graph and never crosses that graph's boundary. Inner edges terminate on Bridge nodes; an outer workspace edge connects the Bridges' projected handles."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-same-node",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q5 · Same node, different graphs",
      "position": {
        "x": -900,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nIf two graph documents declare the same semantic `nodeId`, what changes?\n\n## Answer\n\nThey render inside one semantic-node boundary, but each document remains an ownership seam. Authored edges remain local to their document. Any containing-level relationship still belongs to the containing graph, even when the visual node boundary is shared."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-different-node",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q6 · Different semantic nodes",
      "position": {
        "x": -440,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhat permits an edge between different rendered semantic nodes?\n\n## Answer\n\nThe containing workspace graph authors a regular edge between compatible projected Bridge handles. Opening graphs, following a Portal, or matching adjacency does not itself create that edge."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-portal",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q7 · Do Portals carry edge classes?",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nDo Portals carry edge classes?\n\n## Answer\n\nNo. A Portal identifies and follows a destination Port. Its workspace line is traversal presentation. If a separate gesture authors a relationship, that creates an ordinary edge with its own class at the accepting graph level."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-bridge-class",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q8 · How does a Bridge constrain type?",
      "position": {
        "x": 480,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nHow does a Bridge participate in edge typing?\n\n## Answer\n\nDirection is fixed by the parent edge: source becomes export and target becomes import. The export Bridge reports the edge's actual class. Only the import Bridge declares accepted edge classes and can reject the incoming edge."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "candidate",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Candidate synthesis — not yet law",
      "position": {
        "x": -620,
        "y": 720
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#18302b",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Revised model — implementation target\n\n1. **Every edge is authored at the graph level that owns it.**\n2. **An authored edge never crosses its owning graph/document boundary.**\n3. **At workspace level, rendered graph-nodes are ordinary nodes and their relationship is an ordinary parent edge.**\n4. **When an endpoint graph unfolds, the parent edge extrapolates a Bridge node into that graph.**\n5. **The source endpoint always projects an export Bridge; the target always projects an import Bridge.**\n6. **The export Bridge reports the parent edge's actual class.**\n7. **Only the import Bridge declares accepted classes and validates the incoming edge.**\n8. **Both projections carry the parent edge ID, so pairing is identity-based rather than inferred from adjacency.**\n9. **Portal bindings remain navigational and visually distinct from edges.**\n10. **Same `nodeId` combines graph renderings inside one node boundary without erasing document ownership seams.**"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "counterexample",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Counterexample hunt",
      "position": {
        "x": 80,
        "y": 720
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#38231d",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Questions still open\n\n- Is the current workspace session itself a persistable graph document, or an ephemeral graph awaiting materialization?\n- How does an outer edge retain endpoints when one child graph closes?\n- Can one projected Bridge handle accept multiple outer edges?\n- May inner and outer edges use different classes?\n- How are edge-class constraints declared: allowed set, required class, or typed capability?\n- What happens when a restored workspace cannot load one endpoint graph?\n- When should a workspace graph be promoted to a durable authored graph?"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "plan",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Investigation plan",
      "position": {
        "x": -620,
        "y": 1200
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#1d2940",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Implementation plan\n\n1. Give workspace state its own ordinary typed `edges` collection.\n2. Store endpoints by workspace graph-node instance, graph reference, and handle.\n3. Stop writing newly created outer edges into child declarations.\n4. Derive deterministic export/import Bridge-node projections from each parent edge ID when endpoint graphs are unfolded.\n5. Validate edge class only against the target import capability.\n6. Persist workspace edges with arrangement state and retain legacy `edgeReferences` as a compatibility reader.\n7. Render parent edges through the normal edge-style contract and show derived Bridge projections at unfolded boundaries.\n8. Keep Portal bindings on a separate traversal presentation.\n9. Add materialization only when child-level authoring attaches local structure or changes import policy."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "acceptance",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Decision gate",
      "position": {
        "x": 80,
        "y": 1200
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#2d2440",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Acceptance contract\n\n- No newly authored edge names a node outside its owning graph.\n- Creating an outer edge changes workspace state, not either child graph.\n- Source and target derive export and import Bridges from the same parent edge ID.\n- Export imposes no acceptance policy; import alone accepts or rejects the edge class.\n- Closing a child hides its projection without deleting the workspace edge.\n- Reopening the addressed graph restores the derived Bridge endpoint.\n- Edge class drives the parent edge's workspace rendering.\n- Portal lines cannot be mistaken for authored relationship edges.\n- The same edge abstraction works inside graphs and at workspace scale."
      }
    }
  ],
